 "title": "Brackets in expressions",
 "audience": "learner",
 "keyIdea": "Work out what is inside brackets first. Brackets group numbers together.",
 "explain": {
  "kid": [
   "Brackets (in the US, parentheses) tell you which part to work out first. In 3 × (4 + 2), you add first: 4 + 2 = 6, then 3 × 6 = 18.",
   "Without brackets, 3 × 4 + 2 means 12 + 2 = 14. So brackets can change the answer."
  ],
  "adult": [
   "Grouping symbols mark part of an expression to treat as one number. They are round brackets ( ), square brackets [ ] and braces { }. Work out the innermost group first: 2 × [10 − (3 + 1)] = 2 × [10 − 4] = 2 × 6 = 12.",
   "Brackets let you write exactly the calculation you mean. 'Add 4 and 2, then multiply by 3' is 3 × (4 + 2), not 3 × 4 + 2."
  ]
 },
